Source: tapauth
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Luca Auer <lolle2000.la+tapauth@gmail.com>
# NOTE: the Rust toolchain must be >= 1.85 (Cargo lockfile v4). Debian
# stable archives (e.g. noble) ship older cargo/rustc; builds rely on
# rustup (PATH via debian/rules) or a rust PPA (cargo-1.91/rustc-1.91
# alternates below). Plain `apt-get build-dep` on stock noble is NOT
# sufficient — see scripts/ci/build-debian-packages.sh.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), cargo (>= 1.85) | cargo-1.91, rustc (>= 1.85) | rustc-1.91, protobuf-compiler, libdbus-1-dev, libsystemd-dev, libpam0g-dev, clang, libclang-dev, pkg-config
Standards-Version: 4.7.0

Package: tapauth
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, polkitd | policykit-1, adduser, libdbus-1-3, dbus
Recommends: bluez, iptables
Suggests: firewalld
Description: Local smartphone-based authentication framework
 A modern, privacy-preserving local-first authentication system.
 .
 pam_tapauth.so is wired into sudo, su and polkit-1 only; all other PAM
 stacks (including fingerprint stacks such as kde-fingerprint,
 gdm-fingerprint and fingerprint-auth) stay stock.
 .
 Desktop lock screens and greeters (KDE Plasma, GNOME) can integrate
 through the built-in virtual fprintd D-Bus service
 (net.reactivated.Fprint): stock fingerprint stacks call pam_fprintd.so,
 which resolves to tapauthd. This base package deliberately does NOT
 conflict with the real fprintd packages (fprintd, libpam-fprintd) and
 does NOT ship a D-Bus activation file; tapauthd is a systemd-managed
 daemon that owns the bus name while it runs (and restarts on failure),
 so the virtual bridge needs no activation support from D-Bus. It ships
 only the D-Bus policy file that lets tapauthd own the name.
 .
 The virtual bridge is opt-in. With enable_fprintd_bridge unset (the
 default), it is enabled only when the optional tapauth-fprintd-emulation
 package is installed (it ships the marker file
 /usr/share/tapauth/fprintd-emulation.enabled); the base package ships no
 marker, so a real local fingerprint reader is never shadowed by a base
 install and real fprintd keeps the bus name. Set enable_fprintd_bridge
 = true or = false in /etc/tapauth/config.toml to force the bridge on or
 off explicitly (applies at the next daemon restart). The optional
 tapauth-fprintd-emulation package replaces the fprintd PAM provider
 (libpam-fprintd) with a TapAuth-built pam_fprintd.so and ships the
 marker. No local fingerprint reader is required.

Package: tapauth-fprintd-emulation
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, tapauth (= ${binary:Version})
Conflicts: libpam-fprintd
Replaces: libpam-fprintd
Provides: libpam-fprintd
Description: Optional fprintd PAM emulation for TapAuth (pam_fprintd.so)
 This optional package ships a second build of the TapAuth PAM module,
 installed as pam_fprintd.so, taking over the file normally provided by
 the libpam-fprintd package.
 .
 It exists for the opt-in case where stock fingerprint PAM stacks must be
 served by TapAuth instead of a real local fingerprint reader. It
 Conflicts with, Replaces and Provides libpam-fprintd so the two providers
 of pam_fprintd.so can never coexist; the fprintd daemon package itself is
 deliberately NOT conflicted with.
 .
 It also ships the marker file /usr/share/tapauth/fprintd-emulation.enabled
 which turns the daemon's tri-state enable_fprintd_bridge default into "on"
 (the D-Bus name net.reactivated.Fprint is claimed at tapauthd startup), so
 lock screens/greeters can discover the virtual fingerprint device. Its
 maintainer scripts restart tapauthd so the claim/release takes effect
 immediately. Installed but with enable_fprintd_bridge = false in
 /etc/tapauth/config.toml, the marker is overridden and the bridge stays off.
 .
 The base tapauth package is unchanged: it ships no marker and no D-Bus
 activation file, keeps shipping pam_tapauth.so, and keeps not conflicting
 with fprintd or libpam-fprintd.
 .
 To go back to a real local fingerprint reader, remove this package and
 reinstall libpam-fprintd (and fprintd).
